E=[]
def e(date_iso,date_display,precision,eye,place,headline,digest,quote,conf,note):
    E.append(dict(date_iso=date_iso,date_display=date_display,precision=precision,eye=eye,place=place,headline=headline,digest=digest,quote=quote,confidence=conf,source_note=note))

e("1773-09","September 1773","month","james-burney","Huahine, aboard the Adventure","A Raiatean refugee signs on as a supernumerary",
 "Burney's Adventure journal records the young man who came aboard at Huahine, entered on the books under a garbled form of his name, and soon nicknamed 'Jack' by the crew. Burney credits him with many good qualities and begins learning his language from him.","","approx","Burney's journal (ed. Hooper 1975) as cited in The Burney Journal article; BYUH article for ship's-book name")
e("1773-10","October 1773","month","cook","Tongatapu, aboard the Resolution","Cook's first verdict: an ugly, low-born rogue",
 "At Tonga Cook wrote a dismissive note on Mai in his journal, calling him dark, ugly and a blackguard and doubting he was a fit specimen to take to England. The phrase was softened when the journal was revised for publication.","","approx","Cook's manuscript journal, cited in the Pacific Studies article and the 1977 Auckland catalogue")
e("1774-07-14","14 July 1774","day","mai","Spithead, off Portsmouth","The Adventure brings Mai to England",
 "Reported, not his own words: no surviving firsthand account found. The Adventure anchored at Spithead and Mai, the first Polynesian known to have reached Britain, was taken to London, where Lord Sandwich received him and passed him to Banks.","","approx","Smithsonian Magazine and Pacific Studies article (14 July); the 1977 Auckland catalogue gives 11 July")
e("1774-07-17","Sunday 17 July 1774","day","mai","Kew Palace","Reported greeting to George III",
 "Reported speech: Mai is said to have greeted the King with 'How do, King Tosh', and to have spoken of wanting gunpowder against the Bora Bora men; these sayings come from later retellings, while an eyewitness letter by the young Prince Frederick gives a plain 'How do you do'. The King gave him a sword.","","approx","Smithsonian Magazine; 1977 Auckland catalogue (Prince Frederick's letter); NLA item title for the date")
e("1774-07-17","Sunday 17 July 1774","day","banks","Kew","Banks and Solander present Mai to the King and Queen",
 "Banks brought Mai, still dressed in the fashion of his islands, to Kew with Solander. A picture now in the National Library of Australia carries this date in its title. Banks's letters of these weeks boast of the success his guest was having and of a natural politeness he claimed never to have seen equalled.","","approx","NLA item 'Omiah the Indian from Otaheite presented to their Majesties at Kew by Mr Banks & Dr Solander, July 17, 1774'; Banks's letter cited in Smithsonian Magazine")
e("1774-07","late July 1774","month","fanny","St Martin's Street, London","Her father meets Mai, who calls him 'Bunny'",
 "Fanny later wrote up, with amusement, her father's first encounter with Mai a few days after his arrival, when Mai was still in island dress and was introduced to Dr Burney as the father of his shipmate Jem. Mai's attempt at the family name came out as 'Bunny'.","","approx","Early Journals and Letters II, p. 41, as cited in The Burney Journal article")
e("1774-08-14","14 August 1774","day","sandwich","Hinchingbrooke, Huntingdonshire","Relief that 'our friend Omai' is out of danger",
 "Sandwich thanked his correspondent for news that Mai was out of danger, evidently after his smallpox inoculation by Baron Dimsdale, and sent him compliments. In the same letter he approved terms of £160 a year for the lodging and board of Mai and Mr Andrews (the Adventure's surgeon) and welcomed having him under closer supervision.","","verified","SLNSW transcript of the letter; the inoculation link is inferred from context and secondary accounts")
e("1774-12","late November or early December 1774","month","fanny","St Martin's Street, London","Mai to dinner in a velvet court suit",
 "In a long letter to Samuel Crisp, Fanny describes Mai arriving in a suit of Manchester velvet lined with white satin, wearing the sword the King had given him. She found him darker than expected and not handsome, but praised his bows, his table manners and his wordless courtesy, and set him against Lord Chesterfield's son Philip Stanhope, whom she thought a pedantic booby.","He makes remarkable good bows—not for him but for anybody","verified","Letter to Crisp, Early Journals and Letters II pp. 60–63; quote as printed in Dobson, Fanny Burney (1903)")
e("1774-12","late November or early December 1774","month","mai","St Martin's Street, London","Reported: 'very dood', and twelve ladies to visit",
 "Reported speech, through Fanny Burney: when he spilled beer on his new suit he told her not to trouble herself; he praised the food as 'very dood'; and he counted on his fingers the twelve women he was due to see. Burney recorded his English with care and some mockery.","","approx","Early Journals and Letters II pp. 60–62, as cited in The Burney Journal article")
e("1775-08-14","14 August 1775","day","solander","London, after a trip down the Thames to the Resolution","A ship is promised to carry Mai home",
 "Writing to Banks after a day trip from the Tower past Deptford and Woolwich to see the Resolution, Solander passed on the news that Charles Clerke had been promised command of the ship to carry Mai home. Within a fortnight the Caledonian Mercury printed the same plan.","","verified","SLNSW transcript; Captain Cook Society, July–September 1775 (Caledonian Mercury, 28 Aug 1775)")
e("1775-09","late summer 1775","month","banks","Mulgrave Castle, Yorkshire","Mai taken north to Mulgrave with Phipps",
 "Banks took Mai on a long excursion, by Sandwich's yacht and then to the Yorkshire house of Constantine Phipps, later Lord Mulgrave, while Cook, newly home from the second voyage, was in London. The trip was a show of Mai to country society as much as a holiday.","","approx","Pacific Studies article; 1977 Auckland catalogue")
e("1775-12","late 1775 (date uncertain)","range","fanny","St Martin's Street, London","His English improved, and a song",
 "On a later visit Fanny noted how much English Mai had learned. Asked for a song of his islands, he sang one that struck her as wild and strange, and she joked that it was the only thing savage about him. He also listed for her the London women he thought very pretty.","the only thing that is savage about him","approx","Early Journals and Letters II pp. 193–96; quote as printed in Dobson, Fanny Burney (1903)")
e("1776-02","February 1776","month","cook","London","Cook agrees to take Mai home",
 "After some hesitation Cook accepted command of the new voyage and with it responsibility for returning Mai. In the revised text of his second-voyage journal, prepared for print in these months, he credited Mai's natural good behaviour for his welcome in polite company, a warmer verdict than his 1773 note.","","approx","1977 Auckland catalogue; Pacific Studies article")
e("1776-04-03","Wednesday 3 April 1776","day","boswell","Johnson's house, London","Johnson: so little of the savage in him",
 "Boswell found Johnson putting his books in order and heard him on Mai, whom Johnson had met at dinner at Streatham. Johnson said Mai had kept only the best company, so all he had acquired of English manners was genteel; seated against the light beside Lord Mulgrave, he could hardly tell the two apart.","there was so little of the savage in Omai","verified","Boswell, Life of Johnson (1791), under 3 April 1776")
e("1776-04","early April 1776","month","boswell","London","Boswell talks with Cook about Mai",
 "In the same weeks Boswell met Cook and recorded their conversation about Mai and the coming voyage. The talk reflects a London where the return of Mai was a topic of dinner tables.","","approx","Boswell's journal, as cited in the Pacific Studies article")
e("1776-04","late April 1776","month","reynolds","Royal Academy, Pall Mall","The Portrait of Omai at the Academy",
 "Reynolds's full-length of Mai, robed in white and turbaned, barefoot in an idealised landscape, was one of twelve portraits he showed at the Academy's eighth exhibition and was praised as a good likeness. It was not a commission and stayed in his studio until his death.","","approx","Portrait of Omai article; exhibition month assumed from the Academy's usual spring opening")
e("1776-04","spring 1776","month","fanny","London","Mai's lesson in manners to a duchess",
 "Writing to Crisp, Fanny described seeing the Duchess of Devonshire looking undressed and slatternly, and reported that Mai himself had criticised her appearance; she joked that the duchess needed a lesson in attention from Omai.","","approx","Letter to Crisp, Early Journals and Letters II pp. 203–04, as cited in The Burney Journal article")
e("1776-06-24","24 June 1776","day","mai","London to Chatham","Mai leaves London with Cook",
 "Reported, not his own words: Mai left London with Cook for Chatham to join the Resolution, taking a cargo of gifts that would later include a suit of armour, firearms, a hand organ and other English goods.","","approx","Pacific Studies article")
e("1776-07-12","12 July 1776","day","cook","Plymouth","Resolution sails, Mai aboard",
 "Two days after writing to Banks that Mai had been very much made a fuss of in Plymouth, Cook sailed. The Discovery under Clerke followed later.","","approx","Pacific Studies article (Cook to Banks, 10 July 1776); Smithsonian Magazine")
e("1776-11-26","26 November 1776","day","cook","Cape Town","Mai gives up his cabin to the horses",
 "Writing from the Cape to Sandwich and Banks, Cook reported that livestock had been taken aboard for the islands and that Mai had given up his cabin to make room for horses.","","approx","Pacific Studies article")
e("1777-01-27","27 January 1777","day","cook","Adventure Bay, Van Diemen's Land","Mai fires a musket to impress",
 "At Adventure Bay in Tasmania Mai fired a musket to impress the Aboriginal people who came to the shore, a display Cook recorded. It was the first of several moments where Mai used English arms for standing.","","approx","Pacific Studies article")
e("1777-04-02","2 April 1777","day","james-burney","Atiu (southern Cook Islands)","Mai talks his way through a tense crowd",
 "Burney recorded Mai, detained ashore among a crowd whose mood was uncertain, putting the best face he could on things and demonstrating a fighting method to defuse the tension; he also pretended not to miss a stolen sword. Burney admired his composure.","","approx","Burney's third-voyage journal as quoted in The Burney Journal article 488; place inferred from Cook's track (the article places it in the Friendly Islands)")
e("1777-04","mid-April 1777","month","mai","Palmerston atoll","Reported: a wish to be king of an empty island",
 "Reported speech: at the uninhabited atoll of Palmerston Mai said he would like to become its king, a remark the officers recorded with amusement.","","approx","Pacific Studies article")
e("1777-08-12","12 August 1777","day","gilbert","Vaitepiha Bay, Tahiti","Tears from his sister, quick indifference from others",
 "Gilbert noted the islanders' pleasure at the ships' arrival and Mai's sister greeting him with tears of joy. He added that Tahitians were pleased to hear Mai had been to England, but their curiosity faded within an hour or two, and he judged Mai of low standing.","","verified","Captain Cook Society, July–September 1777")
e("1777-08-12","12 August 1777","day","mai","Vaitepiha Bay, Tahiti","Reported: red feathers buy a welcome",
 "Reported, not his own words: Mai distributed the prized red feathers he had collected at Tonga, and relatives gathered round him. The officers read the welcome as bought.","","approx","Pacific Studies article")
e("1777-08","mid-August 1777","range","cook","Pare (Oparre), Tahiti","Mai in armour on a war canoe",
 "After a mock sea fight staged for the visitors, Mai put on the suit of armour he had been given in England and stood on a platform in a canoe to be seen. Cook also recorded the chief Tu's open dislike of Mai and Mai's imprudent dealings with the chiefs.","Omai put on his suit of armour","approx","1784 official account (1821 reprint); date within mid-August approximate")
e("1777-09-01","1 September 1777","day","cook","Tahiti","Mai at a human sacrifice",
 "Cook went to witness a human sacrifice being offered for war on Moorea, and noted that Mai followed in a canoe. Mai spoke against the rite; the chief Tutaha later gave him a double canoe.","","approx","Captain Cook Society, July–September 1777; Pacific Studies article")
e("1777-09-14","14 September 1777","day","cook","Matavai, Tahiti","Cook and Clerke ride; Mai keeps falling off",
 "Cook and Clerke rode round the plain of Matavai, the first riding the islanders had seen; Mai's earlier attempts had ended with him thrown off. The horse was the most spectacular of the English gifts.","","verified","Captain Cook Society, July–September 1777; 1784 account")
e("1777-09-29","29 September 1777","day","samwell","Matavai Bay, Tahiti","Leaving Tahiti, Mai in his own sailing canoe",
 "Samwell recorded the ships leaving Matavai attended by Mai in his large sailing canoe, the double canoe he had acquired in return for his gifts.","","verified","Captain Cook Society, July–September 1777")
e("1777-10-08","7–9 October 1777","range","gilbert","Moorea","Mai eager to fire on islanders",
 "During Cook's reprisals over a stolen goat, when houses and canoes of suspected thieves were burned, Gilbert wrote that Mai was officious and wanted to fire on the islanders.","","verified","Captain Cook Society, October–December 1777")
e("1777-10-13","13 October 1777","day","cook","Fare, Huahine","A council of chiefs settles Mai's land",
 "With the chiefs assembled, Mai made his offerings and prayers, then asked for land. Cook recorded Mai's claim that his father had been dispossessed by the Bora Bora men and his own refusal to make war; a chief declared the island Cook's to dispose of, and a strip of shore with part of the hill was granted.","","verified","1784 official account; arrival 12 October per Captain Cook Society and Pacific Studies article")
e("1777-10-13","13 October 1777","day","mai","Fare, Huahine","Reported: a plea for his father's land",
 "Reported speech, through Cook: Mai told the chiefs that his father's lands on Raiatea had been seized by Bora Bora conquerors, and asked for help and land. His preferred course, attacking Bora Bora, was refused.","","approx","1784 official account; Pacific Studies article")
e("1777-10-13","about 13 October 1777","day","samwell","Huahine","Horses landed; Mai rides before crowds",
 "Samwell described the horses being landed and Mai riding one, drawing crowds of shouting islanders. It was his most public moment of English status.","","approx","Captain Cook Society, October–December 1777")
e("1777-10-15","15 October 1777","day","james-burney","Huahine","Mai wavers between Huahine and Raiatea",
 "Burney recorded that Mai, having agreed to settle at Huahine, now declared for Raiatea, his home island; within about three days he returned to the Huahine plan.","","verified","Captain Cook Society, October–December 1777")
e("1777-10","October 1777","month","king","Huahine","King: his one wish was war on Bora Bora",
 "King wrote that Mai pressed for little except the destruction of the Bora Bora chiefs and the freeing of his native island, and judged that his English goods would not secure him lasting standing.","","approx","Pacific Studies article")
e("1777-10-22","22 October 1777","day","cook","Huahine","A stolen sextant and a harsh punishment",
 "A sextant was stolen from the astronomer's observatory; Mai helped recover it. Cook had the thief punished severely and set ashore. Two days later a man who destroyed plants in Mai's garden and threatened to kill him and burn his house was seized.","","verified","Captain Cook Society, October–December 1777")
e("1777-10-28","late October 1777","range","gilbert","Huahine","A house of canoe planks and an inventory",
 "Gilbert described the carpenters building Mai a house from canoe planks in about a fortnight, and listed what was left with him: bed, pots and kettles, a hand organ, pistols, a musket, a keg of powder, a horse and a mare with saddle and bridle, and a garden with vines from the Cape.","","approx","Captain Cook Society, October–December 1777")
e("1777-11-02","2 November 1777","day","cook","Huahine","The farewell: composure until the last",
 "As the ships sailed, Mai kept his composure until he came to Cook to say goodbye. Cook left an inscription on the house naming George III and the date, and listed the weapons and animals he left.","He sustained himself with a manly resolution, till he came to me.","verified","1784 official account (1821 reprint); departure date per Captain Cook Society")
e("1777-11-02","2 November 1777","day","king","Huahine","King takes him ashore, weeping",
 "King, who accompanied Mai back to land, reported that he wept the whole way to the shore.","he wept all the time in going ashore","verified","King's report as printed in the 1784 official account")
e("1777-11-02","2 November 1777","day","mai","Huahine","Reported: grief at the parting",
 "Reported, not his own words: no surviving firsthand account found. Officers agreed that Mai broke down at the parting; Lieutenant Williamson's journal records his manly sorrow and tears on reaching Cook.","","verified","Captain Cook Society, October–December 1777 (Williamson); 1784 account")
e("1777-11","November 1777","month","cook","Huahine and Raiatea","Cook's summary: spoiled by England, undecided to the end",
 "In his summing up Cook judged that Mai, much made of in England, had lost sight of his original condition, had remained undetermined to the last about where to live, and might have done better had he chosen his connections more wisely.","lost sight of his original condition","verified","1784 official account, 'summary view of his conduct and character'")
e("1777-11","mid-November 1777","month","mai","Huahine, by messenger to the ships at Raiatea","Reported: a request for a goat and two axes",
 "Reported speech: Mai sent word to the ships at Raiatea that all was well, asking for a replacement goat and two axes, which Cook sent. It is the last message from him recorded by the English.","","approx","Pacific Studies article")
e("1777-03","March 1777","month","forster","London","Forster: England taught him nothing useful",
 "In his account of the second voyage, Forster wrote that Mai had imitated English politeness naturally but had been given no useful knowledge, and that his judgement in choosing goods was in its infancy. He also described his skin as the darkest hue of the common people, reading class into colour.","judgement was in its infant state","approx","A Voyage Round the World (1777) as cited in The Burney Journal article; month of publication approximate")
e("1778-10","October 1778","month","cook","Unalaska","Looking back: Mai left with two horses",
 "Writing from Unalaska in the North Pacific a year later, Cook reported that he had left Mai at Huahine with two horses. It is Cook's last recorded mention of him; Cook was killed in Hawaii in February 1779.","","approx","Pacific Studies article")
e("1788-10-26","Sunday 26 October 1788","day","bligh","Matavai Bay, Tahiti","Bligh hears that Mai is dead",
 "Arriving in the Bounty, Bligh asked after Mai and was told that he and both New Zealand boys left with him were dead. Everyone he asked agreed that they had died a natural death. The report is secondhand.","they died a natural death","verified","Bligh, A Voyage to the South Sea (1792), ch. 6")
